          An air purifier helps with mold the way a mop helps with a leaking pipe. It deals with what has already escaped, and it deals with it well: a true HEPA filter catches nearly every mold spore that passes through it. What it cannot do is remove mold growing on a surface, dry out the wall that mold is living on, or stop a colony from releasing the next batch of spores an hour later. Buy one to lower what you breathe while you fix the source, size it by CADR rather than the box, run it all the time, and never buy anything that makes ozone.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Yes for the Air, No for the Wall
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold has two halves, and only one of them is in the air. The colony itself lives on a surface: drywall paper, the back of a carpet pad, a window frame, a cardboard box in the basement, the inside of a wall cavity. That colony releases spores and fragments into the room, and it releases gases that give mold its musty smell. A purifier works only on the material that leaves the surface and drifts through the machine. Everything still attached to the wall is out of its reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer to 'does an air purifier help with mold' is that it helps with exposure, not with the mold. If you or someone in the house reacts to mold, with stuffy sinuses, an itchy throat or asthma that gets worse in one particular room, then fewer spores in the air is a real and measurable improvement. A well-sized HEPA unit can bring a room's airborne spore load down within an hour of being switched on. That is genuinely useful. It just does not mean the problem has gone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case where a purifier earns its money is clear: a room that has a known moisture issue being fixed, a space that has been remediated and still carries residual spores and odor, a basement that is mold-prone but currently dry, or a bedroom for someone with mold allergies in a humid climate. In each of those, the purifier is one tool among several. The case where it wastes money is just as clear: a purifier parked next to a damp, visibly moldy wall that nobody is fixing. It will run forever and lose.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Moisture first, purifier second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Mold grows where water is. Fix the leak or condensation and bring relative humidity down to 50 percent or below, remove growth you can see, and then use a HEPA purifier to clean up the air. Reverse the order and the purifier is treating symptoms of a problem that is still growing.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a machine, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifiers for mold</a> guide ranks the units we recommend for this job, and the reasoning below explains why those picks lean on true HEPA, high clean air delivery and, for musty rooms, a heavy carbon filter. If you are still deciding between a purifier and a dehumidifier, the short version is that the dehumidifier treats the cause; our <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/">air purifier vs dehumidifier</a> comparison covers when each one wins and when you need both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hepa-vs-spores" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. HEPA vs Mold Spore Size: Why the Filter Is Not the Weak Link
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold spores vary a lot by species, from around 1 micron at the very small end to well over 100 microns for some of the large, multi-celled outdoor types. The genera most often found indoors, such as Aspergillus and Penicillium, produce spores in the low single-digit micron range, and Cladosporium spores are typically somewhat larger. For comparison, a human hair is roughly 50 to 70 microns wide. So most spores are invisible individually, but they are not especially small particles by filtration standards.
          </p>

          <p>
            A true HEPA filter, under the US Department of Energy definition used by most manufacturers, must capture at least <strong>99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns</strong>. That figure is often misread as a cutoff, as if anything smaller slips through. It is the opposite. 0.3 microns is close to the most penetrating particle size, the size that is hardest for a fibrous filter to catch. Larger particles are stopped more easily by impaction and interception, and much smaller ones are caught by diffusion as they bounce around between the fibers. A whole mold spore, at several microns, is captured more reliably than the test particle.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Particle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approximate size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What a true HEPA filter does with it</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large outdoor-type spores (for example Alternaria)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 20 to over 100 microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Captured essentially completely; many settle out of the air before reaching the purifier</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Common indoor spores (Aspergillus, Penicillium)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 2 to 5 microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Captured at better than the 99.97 percent test rating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spore and hyphal fragments</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can be under 1 micron</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Captured efficiently, close to the rated figure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HEPA test particle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.3 microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">99.97 percent minimum by definition</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">MVOCs (musty-smell gases)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Individual molecules</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pass straight through; only activated carbon adsorbs them</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The fragments matter more than most buyers realize. As colonies dry out, get disturbed or age, they shed pieces of hyphae and broken spores, and lab studies have found these fragments can outnumber whole spores in the air. Because they are small, they stay suspended longer and travel deeper into the lungs. True HEPA still catches them well, which is one more reason the label matters. Filters sold as 'HEPA-type', 'HEPA-like' or '99 percent HEPA' have not been held to the 99.97 percent standard and may let considerably more of that fine fraction through.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when people ask about a <strong>HEPA air purifier for mold spores</strong>, the filter itself is rarely the limiting factor. A true HEPA sheet catches almost every spore that passes through it. What limits the real-world result is how much of the room's air actually goes through the filter each hour, which is a question of airflow, sizing and placement rather than filter grade. That is why the rest of this guide spends more time on CADR and run time than on filter marketing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-cant-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What an Air Purifier Can't Do About Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>It cannot remove surface growth.</strong> A colony on drywall, grout, wood or fabric stays there no matter how clean the air gets. The purifier catches what the colony releases, and the colony keeps releasing. The EPA's long-standing guidance is that growth covering roughly 10 square feet or less can usually be cleaned by a homeowner with appropriate protection, and that larger areas, or growth tied to sewage or contaminated water, warrant a professional. Anything porous that is badly colonized, such as drywall or ceiling tile, generally has to be cut out and replaced rather than cleaned.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It cannot reach hidden growth.</strong> Mold behind drywall, under flooring, inside a wall cavity, in a crawl space or in ductwork releases spores and odor that leak into the room through gaps, outlets and seams. The purifier will lower the airborne count in the room, which can actually make the problem harder to notice while the hidden colony keeps spreading. A musty smell that keeps coming back no matter how long the purifier runs is a strong hint that there is growth somewhere you have not found yet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It cannot fix moisture.</strong> Mold needs water, and in most homes that water comes from one of three places: a leak, condensation on cold surfaces, or humidity that sits too high for too long. A purifier does not change humidity at all. Keeping indoor relative humidity at or below about 50 percent is what stops new growth, which is why a correctly set dehumidifier does more to prevent mold than any purifier. Our guide on <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> explains the target, and our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifiers for basements</a> page covers the machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It cannot remove the musty smell with HEPA alone.</strong> That smell comes from microbial volatile organic compounds, or MVOCs, which are gases. Compounds such as geosmin and 1-octen-3-ol are detectable by the human nose at extremely low concentrations, and gas molecules pass through a HEPA filter untouched. Only activated carbon, or a similar adsorbent, holds on to them, and the quantity of carbon is what decides how long that works. A thin carbon-dusted sheet saturates within weeks in a musty room. The heavy carbon beds in units like the Austin Air HealthMate Plus and IQAir HealthPro Plus, both picks in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">mold purifier rankings</a>, exist for exactly this.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It cannot clean spores that have already settled.</strong> Spores land on floors, shelves, upholstery and in carpet, where they wait to be kicked back into the air by footsteps or a vacuum without a sealed HEPA system. A purifier only gets them once they are airborne again. Damp-wiping hard surfaces and using a vacuum with HEPA filtration does more for the settled reservoir than extra purifier hours. If the room is a basement, the full sequence of drying, cleaning and preventing regrowth is laid out in our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">how to get rid of mold in a basement</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CADR Sizing for a Mold Problem: Aim for About Five Air Changes an Hour
          </h2>

          <p>
            CADR, the clean air delivery rate, is the number to size by. It is measured in cubic feet per minute under the AHAM test and published separately for smoke, dust and pollen. For mold, the dust figure is the closest match to spore sizes, but the smoke figure is the most demanding of the three and the one most guidance is written around, so if you size by smoke CADR you will have margin on spores. The square-footage claim on the front of the box is far less reliable because manufacturers calculate it at different air change rates, often as low as two per hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common rule of thumb from AHAM is that a purifier's smoke CADR should be at least two-thirds of the room's floor area in square feet, assuming a ceiling of about 8 feet. Run the numbers and that works out to roughly 5 air changes per hour, which is also the rate we recommend for a room with a known mold concern. A 12 by 15 foot bedroom is 180 square feet, so you want a smoke CADR of 120 or more. Our full walkthrough of the arithmetic, including ceilings higher than 8 feet, is in <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/">what size air purifier do I need</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Floor area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Minimum smoke CADR for about 5 ACH (8 ft ceiling)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small bathroom or closet area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 CFM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom, 12 x 15 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">180 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 CFM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large bedroom or den</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 CFM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Open basement zone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400 CFM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full open basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 670 CFM, usually two units</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Basements are where buyers most often get caught out. A 1,000 square foot basement needs something like 670 CFM of clean air delivery for five air changes, and very few single consumer purifiers publish a smoke CADR anywhere near that. That leaves two honest choices: two mid-size units placed at opposite ends, or one large unit focused on the zone where people actually spend time, with a door or curtain closing off the rest. Coway rates the Airmega 400 for 1,560 square feet at two air changes per hour; scale that to five and it comfortably covers roughly 600 square feet, which is the kind of conversion worth doing for any box claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size up rather than down for two reasons. CADR is measured on the highest fan speed, and few people tolerate maximum speed around the clock. A unit that hits your target on high will likely deliver half of it or less on the speed you actually live with. Second, a mold-prone room has a continuous source, not a one-off puff of smoke, so the purifier is fighting an ongoing release. If you want a broader shortlist of large-room machines outside the mold context, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/">best air purifiers for home</a> guide lists CADR for each pick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="run-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long to Run an Air Purifier for Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run it continuously. The physics explains why. In a well-mixed room with no source, each air change through a perfect filter removes about 63 percent of what is left, so at five air changes an hour the airborne count falls to under 10 percent of its starting level in about 30 minutes, and under 1 percent within an hour. That is the encouraging part. The catch is that a mold-prone room is not a sealed box with a fixed number of spores. The colony, the carpet and the HVAC keep adding more, and the moment the purifier stops, the count starts climbing back.
          </p>

          <p>
            With a continuous source, a purifier does not drive the count to zero. It settles at a steady level where removal matches release, and that level is roughly proportional to the source divided by the total clean air delivery. Double the effective CADR and you roughly halve the steady-state concentration. That is why running on a medium speed all day and night usually beats blasting on high for an hour and then switching off: the average exposure is lower, the noise is tolerable, and you do not spend most of the day back at the starting level.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.3 µm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">HEPA test size, 99.97% capture</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">≈5 ACH</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Target for a mold-prone room</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">≤50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Relative humidity that stops new growth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24/7</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Run time for steady control</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Auto mode is fine if the unit has a decent particle sensor, and units that ramp up when counts spike are useful in basements where running a washer, walking on carpet or opening a door can kick spores loose. Just check what the auto mode settles at. Some machines idle at their lowest speed so aggressively that they deliver a small fraction of their rated CADR for most of the day. If the sensor rarely moves off low in a room you know is musty, set a fixed medium speed instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan for faster filter replacement than the label suggests. Manufacturer intervals assume normal household dust. In a musty room, carbon saturates sooner, and once it is saturated it stops removing odor. Check the HEPA filter monthly for heavy discoloration, and treat a filter that smells musty itself as finished. When you change it, do it outdoors if you can, slide it straight into a bag and seal it, so you are not shaking a season's worth of trapped spores back into the room you just cleaned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ozone-ionizers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ozone Generators, Ionizers and UV-C: The Warning Section
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some devices sold for mold do not filter anything. Ozone generators are marketed as killing mold and erasing musty odors, and the pitch is attractive because it promises to reach the places a filter cannot. The problem is that ozone is a lung irritant. It can cause coughing, chest tightness and throat irritation, and it makes asthma worse, which is a poor trade for a household that bought the device because someone is already reacting to mold. The EPA's position is that ozone at levels that do not exceed public health standards has little effect on most indoor air contaminants, and that it does not effectively remove mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            California regulates this directly. Under the California Air Resources Board regulation that took effect in 2010, indoor air cleaning devices sold in or shipped to California must emit ozone at a concentration under 0.050 parts per million and must be certified. The rule covers more than ozone generators: ionizers, electrostatic precipitators, photocatalytic oxidation units and UV devices all fall under it. CARB publishes a list of certified air cleaners, and checking a model against it is a quick way to rule out the worst offenders, wherever in the country you live.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK BEFORE YOU BUY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If a mold product's main feature is ozone, walk away.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Look the model up on CARB's certified air cleaner list and avoid any device sold as an ozone generator or 'activated oxygen' machine for occupied rooms. Certification means the device stays under the ozone limit. It does not mean the device works, so still demand true HEPA and a published CADR.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Ionizers and plasma-style features sit in a gray zone. Many are certified as low-ozone, and some purifiers include an ionizer as an optional extra on top of a real HEPA filter. The Winix 5500-2 in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">mold purifier rankings</a> is an example; its PlasmaWave feature can be switched off, and that is how we suggest running it for mold. The ionizer adds no proven benefit against spores, independent research on ionizing and oxidizing technologies has raised questions about secondary byproducts, and the HEPA filter is already doing the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            UV-C deserves a calmer verdict. A lamp inside a purifier does not make ozone if it is the right wavelength and the unit is certified, and it does not hurt anything. It also does not do much to mold in the air stream, because air passes the lamp in a fraction of a second and mold spores have tough, pigmented walls that need far longer exposure to inactivate. Treat UV-C as a bonus on a unit you would buy for its HEPA and CADR anyway, never as the reason to buy it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where to Put an Air Purifier for Mold, and the Plan Around It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put it in the room with the problem, not the room you happen to have an outlet in. Within that room, place it toward the area where moisture and spores concentrate, such as near the damp wall, the laundry corner or the bathroom door, but not touching wet surfaces and not on a floor that floods. Give it at least a foot of clear space on every side, and more on the intake side. A purifier pushed into a corner behind a sofa recirculates the same small pocket of air and does a fraction of its rated work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the door if you can. A purifier sized for a 200 square foot room is quickly overwhelmed if it is effectively treating the whole floor through an open doorway, and keeping the space closed also stops spores migrating into the rest of the house. For allergy sufferers, a second, smaller unit in the bedroom is often the single most useful addition, since that is where most people spend eight hours a night. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">best air purifiers for allergies</a> guide covers quiet bedroom units that suit that role.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you clean visible mold, run the purifier on high in that room during and after the work, with the door shut. Scrubbing, scraping and bagging contaminated material releases a burst of spores and fragments, and the purifier is the best tool for catching that burst before it spreads. Wear an N95 respirator, gloves and eye protection, damp the surface before you work on it so less goes airborne, and keep the purifier running for several hours afterward. For larger jobs, professionals use containment and negative air machines for the same reason, at a much bigger scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a basement, pair the purifier with a dehumidifier and treat them as a team. The dehumidifier holds relative humidity at or below 50 percent so the colony stops growing; the purifier handles what is already airborne and the odor. If the basement smells musty, the smell usually fades over several weeks once humidity stays down, which we explain in <a href="/will-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-musty-basement-smell/">will a dehumidifier get rid of musty basement smell</a>. Keep the two machines a few feet apart so neither one's exhaust feeds straight into the other's intake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep a hygrometer in the room. It costs very little, and it tells you whether the actual fix, which is dry air, is working. If humidity stays high despite a dehumidifier, the water is coming from somewhere specific, such as a wall seeping, a drain backing up or a dryer vent leaking into the space. Find and fix that, and the purifier goes back to being what it should be: a finishing tool that keeps the air clean, not a machine fighting a battle it cannot win.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Purifier Instead of Fixing the Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A purifier next to a damp, moldy wall will run around the clock and never get ahead, because the colony keeps releasing spores as long as it has moisture. Find the leak, condensation or high humidity first and bring the room to 50 percent or lower. The purifier is a cleanup and exposure tool, not a repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Ozone Generator Sold as a Mold Killer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone machines promise to reach mold a filter cannot, but ozone irritates lungs and worsens asthma, and the EPA says safe concentrations do little against mold. Check a model against the CARB certified air cleaner list, skip anything sold for continuous ozone output in occupied rooms, and buy true HEPA with a published CADR.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the Square Footage on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage claims are often calculated at two air changes per hour, which is too slow for a room with an active spore source. Use smoke CADR instead, aim for at least two-thirds of the floor area in square feet, and size up again if you will run the unit on a low or medium speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting HEPA to Remove the Musty Smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The musty odor is gas, not particles, so it passes straight through HEPA. Only activated carbon adsorbs it, and a thin carbon sheet saturates within weeks in a musty room. If odor is the main complaint, buy a unit with a substantial carbon bed and replace that carbon more often than the label says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hiding the Unit in a Corner Behind Furniture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A purifier needs clear space on all sides to draw air from the whole room. Tucked behind a couch or into a corner, it cycles the same pocket of air and delivers a fraction of its rated output. Put it in the open, near the moisture source, with a foot or more of clearance.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers help with mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for airborne spores. A true HEPA air purifier captures at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, and nearly all mold spores are larger than that, so it can sharply reduce what you breathe in a room. It does not remove mold growing on surfaces, fix moisture, or kill a colony. Use it alongside source control, not instead of it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an air purifier kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any practical sense. HEPA purifiers trap spores rather than kill them, and the spores stay in the filter until you replace it. UV-C stages inside purifiers expose air for too short a time to reliably inactivate tough mold spores. Devices that claim to kill mold with ozone do so only at concentrations that are unsafe to breathe, so avoid them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an air purifier help with black mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps with the airborne part, just as with any mold. Stachybotrys, often called black mold, has spores that are sticky and tend to become airborne mainly when growth dries out or is disturbed, and a true HEPA filter catches them easily. Visible black mold still needs to be removed and the moisture fixed. Large areas call for a professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take an air purifier to remove mold spores?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a well-sized room at about five air changes per hour, airborne spore counts can drop by more than 90 percent within roughly half an hour, assuming good air mixing. Because mold-prone rooms keep releasing new spores, counts rebound when the unit stops. That is why continuous operation is recommended rather than a short daily session.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I run an air purifier while cleaning mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Scrubbing or cutting out moldy material releases a burst of spores and fragments, and running a HEPA purifier on high in the closed room during and for several hours after the work catches much of it. Wear an N95 respirator, gloves and goggles, dampen the surface first, and bag debris before carrying it through the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an air purifier make mold worse?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A HEPA purifier will not make mold grow, but two things can backfire. A filter left in a damp space for too long can become a reservoir of trapped spores and musty odor, so replace filters on schedule and handle them carefully. And purifiers that emit ozone can irritate lungs and react with indoor chemicals, which is why CARB caps their ozone output.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an air purifier or a dehumidifier better for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dehumidifier is better at preventing mold, because it removes the moisture mold needs and keeps humidity at or below 50 percent. An air purifier is better at reducing the spores and odor already in the air. In a damp basement, fix the moisture first and add a purifier second. Many mold-prone homes are best served by running both together.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

