        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Air purifiers do help with mold, but only with the part that is floating in the air. A true HEPA filter catches nearly every spore that reaches it, so the real questions are whether enough air reaches it, which comes down to CADR, run time and placement, and whether anything is being done about the colony and the moisture behind it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Size the unit for about five air changes an hour using smoke CADR, run it continuously, put it in the open near the source, add a heavy carbon filter if the room smells musty, and never buy a machine whose selling point is ozone. Then fix the water. If you are ready to pick a unit, start with our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifiers for mold</a>.
          </p>
